 This is not exactly OAuth2 login in the normal browser sense, because:

 the user is already inside Telegram, Telegram sends signed user data directly
 your backend verifies the signature with the bot token
 So the most accurate name is:
 Telegram Mini App authentication using initData verification
 then
 JWT authentication for API requests
1) Identity proof from Telegram
2) Session/API access with JWT

-------------------------------
JwtEncoder and JwtDecoder come from Security OAuth2 JOSE support.
we write them manually on Mosh project!
------------------------------

1)the user come from telegram and login.
2)backend create a AuthenticatedUser based on this verified user
3)backend create a JWT token based on this AuthenticatedUser

initData -> TelegramUser -> Owner -> AuthenticatedUser -> JWT
                                                          (use some dependency for creating JWT)
------------------------------
 @AuthenticationPrincipal extract data from security context!
 so it will PREVENT
 manually parse the JWT in every controller / manually look up the logged-in user every time
 it will inject the currently authenticated user (principal)
 into this method parameter.(controller methods)
 So the annotation is just Spring saying:
 "take the principal out of the SecurityContext and inject it here."
                                                  client sends "Authorization: Bearer <JWT>"
                                                          ↓
                                                  JwtAuthenticationFilter  →  parses + validates the JWT, builds an AuthenticatedUser
                                                          ↓                         (accountId, telegramUserId, role, displayName)
                                                  SecurityContext  ←  stores it for the duration of the request
                                                          ↓
                                                  controller method receives it via @AuthenticationPrincipal AuthenticatedUser owner

  Why it matters: identity comes from the verified token, never from the request body.
                  Without this pattern, a controller might take an ownerId from JSON — and then any client could
                  pretend to be any owner.
                  With @AuthenticationPrincipal, the client only controls the token;
                  the filter controls who the token says they are.

  The manual equivalent would be:
  AuthenticatedUser owner = (AuthenticatedUser) SecurityContextHolder.getContext()
          .getAuthentication().getPrincipal();

--------------------------------
why we need Filter part?
imagine we have a login user (he has valid token)
The filter runs on each request and does something like this:
1.Read Authorization: Bearer <token>
2.Extract the JWT
3.Validate it
4.Parse it into AuthenticatedUser
5.Put that user into Spring Security’s SecurityContext. (populates the SecurityContext)

so every request will passed from this filter chain
so we can extract the token from header, validate it, and populates the SecurityContext
automatically.